<title>BRAZIL: Brazil coffee exporters ask to import cheaper bags.</title>
<headline>Brazil coffee exporters ask to import cheaper bags.</headline>
<dateline>RIO DE JANEIRO 1996-10-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's Coffee Exporters' Federation (FEBEC) Thursday asked Industry and Commerce Minister Francisco Dornelles to allow coffee exporters to import jute bags without a minimum import price, according to a FEBEC statement.</p>
<p>This is because domestic producers are asking for $1.60 a bag against the $0.60 sought by producers in such countries as India and Bangladesh, it said.</p>
<p>The Brazilian jute bag industry has benefitted from a decision by the federal government in 1992 to protect it from dumping.</p>
<p>Currently, coffee exporting companies which wish to import jute bags can only do so by paying a minimum import price which corresponds to $2.50 a bag, FEBEC said.</p>
<p>The current price paid by exporters for Brazilian jute bags is contributing to a higher &quot;Brazil cost&quot; and contradicts the policy of helping exports as when the state export tax was lifted, it added.</p>
<p>--Caroline Bulloch, Rio de Janeiro newsroom, 55215074151</p>
